The song:
- Each verse has 4 lines:
  1. "{N} green bottles hanging on the wall,"
  2. "{N} green bottles hanging on the wall,"
  3. "And if one green bottle should accidentally fall,"
  4. "There'll be {n-1} green bottle(s) hanging on the wall."
- When N=1, it's "One green bottle hanging on the wall," (singular), and "There'll be no green bottles hanging on the wall."
- When N=2, it's "Two green bottles... There'll be one green bottle hanging on the wall." (singular "bottle")
- Verses are separated by a blank line "" between them.

Number words: 0 = "no", 1 = "one", 2 = "two", 3 = "three", 4 = "four", 5 = "five", 6 = "six", 7 = "seven", 8 = "eight", 9 = "nine", 10 = "Ten".

Note the capitalization: the first letter of the count word is capitalized at the start of a line ("Ten", "Nine", etc.), but in "There'll be nine green bottles..." it's lowercase. The test file has a `Title` helper, which suggests the intended solution uses `Title` to capitalize. But I can just capitalize manually. The `Title` function is defined in the test file — if I define my own, it would conflict. I can use the test's `Title` function, but that's a bit hacky. Better to implement my own capitalization. Actually, the test file defines `Title` in the same package, so I can call it, but it's cleaner not to depend on it. I'll just capitalize the first rune myself.

